The question asks whether PostgreSQL uses SAVEPOINT to roll back part of the statements inside a transaction.

Yes, this is true. In PostgreSQL (and SQL in general), a SAVEPOINT marks a named checkpoint within a transaction. If you want to roll back only a portion of a transaction (not the entire transaction), you can use ROLLBACK TO <savepoint_name>. This undoes the work done since that savepoint without rolling back the entire transaction.

For example:
BEGIN;
SAVEPOINT my_savepoint;
INSERT ...;
ROLLBACK TO my_savepoint; -- only rolls back the INSERT
COMMIT;

So the answer is "yes".

